sweet-epoch-bootarg (lowclk 2026-11-13, docs/research/47-iortc-wait.md option a1): the platform clock reads boot-arg sweetepoch=<unix seconds, decimal> + sleepless uptime, so the
calendar is the host's date instead of 1970 + uptime [obs: patch 0123 only publishes IORTC, getGMTTimeOfDay is the IOPlatformExpert default and returns 0; TLS validation and some daemons
see 1970]. Overrides SweetPlatformExpert::getGMTTimeOfDay / setGMTTimeOfDay (iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPlatformExpert.cpp). Why there [read xnu-8796.141.3]: clock_initialize_calendar
(osfmk/kern/clock.c:1052, called by IOKitInitializeTime from bsd_init before launchd) reads PEGetUTCTimeOfDay -> IOPlatformExpert::getUTCTimeOfDay -> getGMTTimeOfDay
(iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp:493-498, 1361-1372) and derives clock_calend/boottime from it, so every later clock_get_calendar_microtime / gettimeofday reader sees the epoch;
settimeofday goes clock_set_calendar_microtime -> PESetUTCTimeOfDay -> setGMTTimeOfDay (clock.c:826), which stores base = secs - uptime, so userland can still set the time (the clock is not pinned).
The boot-arg is parsed once, on the first call (the calendar init), as a plain decimal string (PE_parse_boot_arg_str): hex/octal/negative/junk are refused. Range 1704067200 <= v < 4102444800 (2024..2100).
Log: success `SWEET TIME: calendar set from sweetepoch=<v> (host clock at boot image build; drift = boot delay)`; absent `SWEET GAP: no sweetepoch boot-arg (calendar stays at 1970 + uptime)`;
bad value `SWEET GAP: sweetepoch=<v> out of range, calendar stays at 1970 + uptime` (or `is not a decimal number`) and getGMTTimeOfDay keeps returning 0 as before.
Patch 0123's line `SWEET RTC: published IORTC resource, no time source (calendar 1970 + uptime)` is unchanged and is printed from start() before this; with a valid boot-arg the SWEET TIME line that follows supersedes it.
Not handled: sleep/wake (clock_get_system_microtime excludes sleep; the port does not sleep, research 47 s1). Host test: tools/test_epoch_core.py (compiles the SWEET_EPOCH_CORE block of this patch). No hardware access. ORDER: after 0123, 0200.
